\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(0,0,0,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":11.689497716894984}},\"hovermode\":\"closest\",\"barmode\":\"relative\"},\"config\":{\"doubleClick\":\"reset\",\"modeBarButtonsToAdd\":[\"hoverclosest\",\"hovercompare\"],\"showSendToCloud\":false,\"displayModeBar\":false},\"source\":\"A\",\"attrs\":{\"22b46c5cf13\":{\"yintercept\":{},\"type\":\"scatter\"},\"22b465e8c6c\":{\"x\":{},\"y\":{},\"text\":{}}},\"cur_data\":\"22b46c5cf13\",\"visdat\":{\"22b46c5cf13\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"],\"22b465e8c6c\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"]},\"highlight\":{\"on\":\"plotly_click\",\"persistent\":false,\"dynamic\":false,\"selectize\":false,\"opacityDim\":0.20000000000000001,\"selected\":{\"opacity\":1},\"debounce\":0},\"shinyEvents\":[\"plotly_hover\",\"plotly_click\",\"plotly_selected\",\"plotly_relayout\",\"plotly_brushed\",\"plotly_brushing\",\"plotly_clickannotation\",\"plotly_doubleclick\",\"plotly_deselect\",\"plotly_afterplot\",\"plotly_sunburstclick\"],\"base_url\":\"https://plot.ly\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThis line shows whether paychecks are beating prices. Above zero, the average worker's pay is growing faster than\nprices; below zero, it's falling behind. From mid-2023 through 2025, pay comfortably outran prices. In\nOctober 2026, it dipped below zero: **-0.3%**.\n\n## The Fed is raising rates again\n\nTo fight inflation, the Federal Reserve raises interest rates, which makes borrowing more expensive and cools\nspending. After holding rates steady for most of 2026, the Fed has started nudging them up: the federal funds rate\nrose from **3.63%** to **3.88%** this fall, and many forecasters expect another increase in December.\nFor households, that means **higher rates on credit cards, car loans, and mortgages.**\n\n## The fine print\n\n- **Inflation numbers are averages.** If you drive a lot, energy inflation hits you much harder than someone who\n  works from home.\n- **\"Core\" inflation** leaves out food and energy because they swing wildly; it's a better guide to the long-term\n  trend, but you still pay for gas and groceries.\n- **Pay is the average hourly earnings** of production and nonsupervisory workers, before taxes.\n- **This can change quickly.** If oil prices fall (as they did briefly this month on hopes of calmer fighting with\n  Iran), headline inflation could ease fast.\n\n## The bottom line\n\nInflation is back, but it's a different animal than 2022: so far it's mostly an energy shock, with everything else\nrising at a more normal pace. That's the good news. The bad news is that it's enough to put paychecks behind prices\nagain, and the Fed's response means borrowing is getting more expensive too.\n\n---\n\n**How we did this.** All figures are from the Bureau of Labor Statistics and the Federal Reserve via\n[FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/): CPI for all urban consumers (CPIAUCSL), CPI less food and energy (CPILFESL),\nenergy (CPIENGSL), food (CPIUFDSL), shelter (CUSR0000SAH1), gasoline (CUSR0000SETB01), producer prices for final\ndemand (PPIFIS), average hourly earnings of production and nonsupervisory employees (AHETPI), and the effective\nfederal funds rate (FEDFUNDS). Changes are compared with the same month a year earlier.\n",
